Align the echo controller again when PulseAudio starts or moves a stream by itself

Patch 0014 aligns the echo controller again when an audio device module marks that it
started recording again by itself in a call (MarkAudioDeviceRestarted). PulseAudio's
module does that in three places, none of them marked so far:
- Restore (patch 0006): the sound server came back, and the device starts again the
  playout and recording the call had asked for, on new streams with another delay
  between them;
- FollowChosen (patch 0006): the chosen output or input, gone and replaced by the
  server's default meanwhile, is listed again, and the device stops and starts that
  stream on it;
- a stream the server moves by itself to another device (PaStreamMovedCallback: the
  user's choice in the system's mixer, or the device gone), which does not stop it but
  sends what is played, or takes what is recorded, through another path.
Each now marks it. Restore and FollowChosen mark only when recording runs for the call
once they are done (recording was asked for and started); the moved stream is marked
as it comes, from PulseAudio's thread, whether recording runs or not, and a start of
recording that follows (an unmute) takes the count as seen, so it costs nothing then.
The audio processing aligns the echo controller again at the next recorded block.

Two consequences, both harmless. With no sound server at a call's start, recording asked
for is started later by Restore (patch 0012's deferred start), which marks it: one
realign of an echo controller that has processed nothing yet. A device going away moves
both of the module's streams (playout and recording), each marked: two realigns a few
milliseconds apart when they fall on different recorded blocks.

Windows' Core Audio module (AudioDeviceWindowsCore) has nothing to mark: it never starts
its streams again by itself while recording runs. A change of the system's default device
or an endpoint's state is told (patches 0003 and 0011) but the streams stay on the
endpoint they were opened on; a stall of capture events is waited through (patch 0013)
and a stream that fails ends the capture thread for good. A restart there is the
wrapper's, for a device chosen, which wrapper patch 0026 marks.

Measured in the Linux call lab (tools/call-lab/device-echo.sh: the other side talking, a's
outputs looped back into its microphone 60 and 180 ms later; before, without patches 0014
and 0015, against after, interleaved; "over" is the time in the first 2 s more than 20 dB
over the echo's floor before the event, medians [range]):
- the sound server stopped and started again (12 events each): marked once and aligned
  once at each return; before 1.2 s [0.75, 1.45] at -20 dBFS at the loudest, a burst at
  every return (up to 1.55 s), after 0.1 s [0.05, 0.3] at -37 dBFS, bursts of 0.2 to 0.4
  s in 3 of 12;
- a's playback moved by the server to the other looped sink (12 each): each move marked
  once (two events found the stream there already, the application's own output keeper
  having moved it back to the chosen output, a move marked once too); before 1.1 s
  [0, 1.35] at -16 dBFS, bursts in 10 of 12 (up to 1.3 s), after 0.1 s [0, 0.2] at -28
  dBFS, one burst of 0.25 s;
- a's recording moved to another source carrying the same microphone (12 each): marked
  once each; before 0.15 s [0, 0.85], bursts in 5 of 12, after 0.1 s [0.05, 0.15] and no
  burst, but at -25 dBFS at the loudest where before most moves let nothing through (-67
  at the median): the realign itself lets 50 to 150 ms of echo through where the path
  did not change;
- a's output taken away (its playback moved by the server to the default output, looped
  too) and made again (moved back) (24 events each): marked once each; before 0.5 s
  [0, 1.75] at -17 dBFS, bursts in 19 of 24 (up to 2.15 s), after 0.05 s [0, 0.3] at -28
  dBFS, bursts in 3 of 24 (up to 0.45 s). Both moves were the server's or the
  application's, not FollowChosen's restart;
- FollowChosen: the sound server stopped and started again with a's chosen output
  missing for 6 s (its playback started again on the default output, not looped), then
  listed again (12 events each): the move back to it marked once and aligned once (with
  Restore's mark before it); before 0.38 s [0.1, 0.75] at -29 dBFS, bursts in 9 of 12 (up
  to 0.6 s), after 0.05 s [0, 0.3] at -68 dBFS at the loudest, faint bursts (under -50
  dBFS) in 5 of 12.

--- a/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
+++ b/modules/audio_device/linux/audio_device_pulse_linux.cc
@@ -1605,6 +1605,7 @@
     return;
   }
   bool failed = false;
+  bool restarted = false;
   restoring_ = true;
   if (play_wanted_ != Wanted::kNo && !_playing) {
     // The chosen output where it is still listed, else the default until it is.
@@ -1614,6 +1615,8 @@
     if (InitPlayout() != 0 ||
         (play_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && StartPlayout() != 0)) {
       failed = true;
+    } else {
+      restarted = play_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted;
     }
   }
   if (rec_wanted_ != Wanted::kNo && !_recording) {
@@ -1623,9 +1626,16 @@
     if (InitRecording() != 0 ||
         (rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && StartRecording() != 0)) {
       failed = true;
+    } else {
+      restarted = restarted || rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted;
     }
   }
   restoring_ = false;
+  // Fogata, patch 0015: recording started again by the device itself while the
+  // call still records (the server back): the echo controller aligns again.
+  if (restarted && rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && _recording) {
+    MarkAudioDeviceRestarted();
+  }
   if (failed) {
     const TimeDelta delay = RestoreDelay(round);
     RTC_LOG(LS_WARNING) << "PulseAudio: playout or recording did not start "
@@ -1648,6 +1658,7 @@
     return;
   }
   bool failed = false;
+  bool moved = false;
   restoring_ = true;
   if (play_on_default_ && _playing) {
     const uint16_t index = DeviceIndexOf(false, play_name_);
@@ -1659,6 +1670,8 @@
       if (InitPlayout() != 0 ||
           (play_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && StartPlayout() != 0)) {
         failed = true;
+      } else {
+        moved = play_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted;
       }
     }
   }
@@ -1672,10 +1685,17 @@
       if (InitRecording() != 0 ||
           (rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && StartRecording() != 0)) {
         failed = true;
+      } else {
+        moved = moved || rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted;
       }
     }
   }
   restoring_ = false;
+  // Fogata, patch 0015: playout or recording started again on the chosen device,
+  // back, while the call records: the echo controller aligns again.
+  if (moved && rec_wanted_ == Wanted::kStarted && _recording) {
+    MarkAudioDeviceRestarted();
+  }
   // Moved and not started again: tried again as after the server came back. What
   // was not running before is already Restore's, which looks for the chosen device
   // by name at each try.
@@ -1887,6 +1907,11 @@
   RTC_LOG(LS_INFO) << "PulseAudio: a stream moved to another device: audio "
                       "devices changed";
   MarkAudioDevicesChanged();
+  // Fogata, patch 0015: the server moved a stream of this device's to another
+  // device (by the user's choice in its mixer, or a device gone): what is played
+  // reaches the microphone by another path, and the echo controller aligns again
+  // at the next recorded block (taken as seen by a start of recording).
+  MarkAudioDeviceRestarted();
 }
 
